Assume 32-bit `int`. What does this program print? (`<<` and `>>` are **shift operators**.)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
int main(void)
{
    uint8_t flags = 0;
    flags |= 1 << 3;
    flags |= 1;
    printf("%d %d\n", flags, (flags >> 3) & 1);
    return 0;
}
```

Assume 32-bit `int`. What two lines does this program print?

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
int main(void)
{
    uint8_t v = 0xFF;
    v &= ~(1 << 4);
    printf("%d\n", v);
    v ^= 0x0F;
    printf("0x%X\n", v);
    return 0;
}
```

Assume 32-bit `int`. This code is meant to test whether bit 2 of `flags` is set. What does it actually print, and why?

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
int main(void)
{
    uint8_t flags = 4;
    if (flags & 0x04 == 0x04)
        printf("bit 2 set\n");
    else
        printf("bit 2 clear\n");
    return 0;
}
```

cards.body:
A `switch` jumps to the `case` label whose constant equals the switched integer value, and `default` catches everything else.

```c
switch (n) {
    case 1: ...; break;
    case 2: ...; break;
    default: ...;
}
```

A **case label** is the `case 1:` line: a constant value followed by a colon. The `switch` evaluates `n` once, jumps to the matching label, and keeps running from there. The `break` statement leaves the `switch`.

Why: A `switch` is tidier than a long `else if` chain when you compare one integer, or a `char`, against many fixed values, such as command codes or button numbers.

Watch out: The labels must be constants, and the switched value must be an integer type. You cannot switch on a string or a `double`.

