{
    for (int row = 1; row <= 3; row++) {
        for (int col = 0; col < row; col++)
            printf("#");
        printf("\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

cards.body:
C passes arguments by value: the function receives copies, so changing a parameter changes only the function's own copy.

```c
void add_ten(int n) { n = n + 10; }
/* in main: */
int score = 5;
add_ten(score);   /* score is still 5 */
```

When `add_ten(score)` runs, the value `5` is copied into the parameter `n`. The function adds 10 to its copy, and when the function returns, the copy disappears. The variable `score` in the caller was never touched.

Key idea: To get a result back, use the return value: `score = add_ten(score);` with a function that does `return n + 10;`.

Watch out: This is why a `swap(a, b)` function written with two plain `int` parameters can never swap anything. Later lessons show how to give a function access to the caller's own variable.

cards.title:
What number does this program print? (A local variable declared `static` is a **static variable**.)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int next_id(void)
{
    static int id = 0;
    id++;
    return id;
}
int main(void)
{
    next_id();
    next_id();
    printf("%d\n", next_id());
    return 0;
}
```

These two files are compiled together. What does the program print, and what does the `extern` line do?

```c
/* counter.c */
int hits = 0;
void hit(void) { hits++; }

/* main.c */
#include <stdio.h>
extern int hits;
void hit(void);
int main(void)
{
    hit(); hit();
    printf("%d\n", hits);
    return 0;
}
```

