cards.body:
`==` compares where the two arrays live in memory, not their characters, so two separate arrays with identical text still compare as different; use `strcmp`.

```c
char a[] = "cat";
char b[] = "cat";
/* a == b is 0 (false); strcmp(a, b) == 0 is 1 (true) */
```

Two arrays are two separate blocks of memory, even if they hold the same text, and `==` asks only whether they are the same block. A compiler with `-Wall` may warn that the comparison "always evaluates to false".

Key idea: To compare string contents, call `strcmp(a, b)` from `string.h`. It returns `0` when the strings are equal. To copy a string, use a copy function, not `=`.

Tip: In Python `==` compares text, so this is an easy habit to carry over by mistake.

cards.title:
What does this program print? (`strcmp` returns `0` for equal strings, a negative number if the first string sorts earlier, and a positive number if it sorts later.)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main(void)
{
    printf("%d %d\n",
           strcmp("cat", "cat") == 0,
           strcmp("Zebra", "apple") < 0);
    return 0;
}
```

What does this program print, and why is the line `buf[3] = '\0';` needed?

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main(void)
{
    char buf[4];
    strncpy(buf, "hello", 3);
    buf[3] = '\0';
    printf("%s %zu\n", buf, strlen(buf));
    return 0;
}
```

What does this program print? (`ctype.h` provides `isdigit` and `toupper`.)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <ctype.h>
int main(void)
{
    char word[] = "c2d";
    for (int i = 0; word[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        if (isdigit(word[i]))
            printf("%d", word[i] - '0');
        else
            printf("%c", toupper(word[i]));
    }
    printf("\n%d\n", 'a' - 'A');
    return 0;
}
```

